---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import SpecTable from '~/components/SpecTable.astro';
import SectionHead from '~/components/SectionHead.astro';

/**
 * Every figure here should be something you have actually measured or can
 * point at. A /uses page full of aspirational kit is worse than no /uses page.
 */
const cluster = [
  { k: 'Nodes', v: '6 × Raspberry Pi 5 8GB' },
  { k: 'Storage', v: '6 × 1TB NVMe' },
  { k: 'Network', v: '2.5GbE managed, PoE+' },
  { k: 'Distribution', v: 'k3s' },
  { k: 'CNI', v: 'Cilium' },
  { k: 'GitOps', v: 'Flux' },
  { k: 'Idle draw', v: '~48W' },
];

const printers = [
  { k: 'Machines', v: '3, all Klipper' },
  { k: 'Largest volume', v: '350 × 350 × 350' },
  { k: 'Materials', v: 'PLA, PETG, ASA, TPU' },
  { k: 'Slicer', v: 'OrcaSlicer' },
  { k: 'Monitoring', v: 'Moonraker → Prometheus' },
];

const desk = [
  { k: 'Editor', v: 'Neovim' },
  { k: 'Shell', v: 'zsh + tmux' },
  { k: 'Terminal', v: 'Ghostty' },
  { k: 'Font', v: 'IBM Plex Mono' },
  { k: 'Languages', v: 'Go, TypeScript' },
];

const thisSite = [
  { k: 'Framework', v: 'Astro 5' },
  { k: 'Content', v: 'MDX + Zod schemas' },
  { k: 'Styling', v: 'Plain CSS, no framework' },
  { k: 'Client JS', v: 'None' },
  { k: 'Hosting', v: 'Cloudflare, static' },
  { k: 'Build', v: 'Push to main' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Uses"
  description="The hardware I run, the printers I maintain, and the tools I work in — with what I'd change."
  section="uses"
>
  <article class="post-wrap">
    <h1>Uses</h1>
    <p class="post-lede">
      What I actually run, rather than what I'd recommend. Most of it has been in service long
      enough to have opinions attached, so those are here too.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <SectionHead label="Cluster" moreHref="/projects/pi-tower/" moreLabel="pi-tower" />
      <SpecTable rows={cluster} />
      <p class="verdict">
        Six nodes rather than three, so I can drain two for maintenance and still have quorum.
        That sounds like over-provisioning until the first time you'd otherwise have postponed
        an upgrade for a fortnight.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict">
        <strong>What I'd change:</strong> nothing about the topology, everything about the
        order I did it in. NVMe boot was worth more than every tuning change I made before it,
        and I made a lot of them first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <SectionHead label="Printers" moreHref="/projects/printers/" moreLabel="printers" />
      <SpecTable rows={printers} />
      <p class="verdict">
        All three run Klipper, which is less about speed than about the machine being
        introspectable — config in a text file, macros I can read, and metrics I can scrape.
        A printer I can't query is a printer I can't debug.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict">
        <strong>What I'd change:</strong> I'd have consolidated on one toolhead ecosystem
        earlier. Three machines with three different hotends means three sets of spares and
        three sets of habits.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <SectionHead label="Desk" />
      <SpecTable rows={desk} />
      <p class="verdict">
        Boring on purpose. Everything here is something I can reproduce on a fresh machine
        from a dotfiles repo in about ten minutes, which matters more to me than any individual
        tool being optimal.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <SectionHead label="This site" />
      <SpecTable rows={thisSite} />
      <p class="verdict">
        Static, no client JavaScript, no framework CSS. Content is MDX validated against Zod
        schemas at build time, so a malformed post fails CI rather than shipping broken.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict">
        <strong>Deliberately not self-hosted.</strong> It would be neater to serve this from
        the cluster it talks about, but then an evening of tinkering takes the portfolio down
        with it. The cluster runs the things that need to be running.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
